cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use these kernels, cite Thiery, Michel and Chen, whose biharmonic coordinates they restate.
title: Cage kernels (Lean-emitted) -- the (1,3) biharmonic bind and the in-motion cage fit
abstract: >-
  lean/Cage/SlangCodegen/*.lean restate, as LeanSlang compute kernels, the
  (1,3) biharmonic coordinates for triangular 3D cages as src/BHC.h of
  V-Sekai-fire/interactor-tool-godot-cage-deformer writes them
  (get_signed_solid_angle, h_coordinates and its inside-triangle case,
  bh_psi, bh_psi_gradient_in_tangent_plane, bh_phi, and
  computeConstrainedBiharmonicMatrices_13 with its default gamma_D = 1 and
  three subdivision levels), with the same expression order, in double.
  Local adaptations: log and atan2 are series in +, -, x, /, sqrt and frexp
  (SPIR-V's GLSL.std.450 Log and Atan2 take no 64-bit operands); Eigen's
  inverse() is replaced by an in-place LU with partial pivoting and one
  solve per right-hand side, on the normal equations G = A^T A + B^T B with
  the right-hand side -A^T B (the Laplacian and Dirichlet samples coincide,
  so R = -B exactly); the blend is w = h + X^T bh per point. No BHC.h source
  is copied. The fit's kernels (normals and their VJP, the uniform Laplacian,
  per-frame bone blend, LBS and its transpose, the body's pseudonormals,
  the closest-point hinge, df32 sums, the winding number) are written here.
  lake exe emit_cage writes the Slang, the pins and CageKernelTable.inc;
  gen.sh compiles cpp/ and SPIR-V and embed_spv.cmake embeds it (no Python).
  Checked against the native BHC.h oracle (gates/9-cage, G1).
type: software
